Protein stability is a fundamental property governing cellular function. Predicting the stability of novel proteins remains a challenge due to the complexity of the native state. Recent advancements in protein folding prediction have utilized deep learning to estimate the probability of a protein folding into its native state. This report presents a comparative study of two prediction methods: a traditional statistical classification approach and a novel deep learning architecture.

\section{Baseline Classification Model}
The baseline classifier employs support vector machines (SVMs) trained on amino acid sequence features. Features include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Atom-wise Helix Index} (H-index): Measures local hydrophobicity.
\item \textit{Surface Area} (SA): Reflects solvent accessibility.
\end{itemize}
The hypothesis is that features derived from local structural elements (H-index and SA) correlate strongly with global stability.

